/// The opt-in session retention sweeper (gh-1746).
///
/// Pure Dart over [FileSystem]: walks the sessions root stat-first, plans
/// oldest-last-used-first deletions under the cap, and (on apply) deletes
/// whole units — the transcript, its rotated segments, the `.tools` scope
/// file, the per-session usage directory, and the workspace spills —
/// re-checking presence immediately before every unlink (E2).
final class SessionRetentionSweeper {
  // The sweeper's filesystem/root params read better under their public
  // names than as `_fs`/`_root` initializing formals (the repo's
  // established ignore, see checkpoint_tool.dart).
  // ignore_for_file: prefer_initializing_formals

  /// Creates a sweeper over [fs] rooted at [sessionsRoot].
  SessionRetentionSweeper({
    required FileSystem fs,
    required String sessionsRoot,
    this.retention = const SessionRetentionConfig(),
    this.presenceStore,
    this.currentSessionId,
    DateTime Function()? now,
    this.log,
    this.cleanWorkspaceSpills = true,
    this.softWarnBytes = retentionSoftWarnBytes,
  }) : _fs = fs,
       _root = sessionsRoot,
       _now = now ?? DateTime.now;

  /// The filesystem abstraction (never `dart:io`).
  final FileSystem _fs;

  /// The sessions root.
  final String _root;

  /// The opt-in policy; the default constructs to no cap at all.
  final SessionRetentionConfig retention;

  /// Live-session heartbeats; null disables the live exemption (tests
  /// without presence).
  final SessionPresenceStore? presenceStore;

  /// The session this process owns — never a deletion candidate.
  final String? currentSessionId;

  /// Clock seam (tests).
===
7:One sentence: **the sessions root gets an opt-in retention policy (`sessions.retention`) — a total-size cap enforced by an age-aware, live/named-safe sweep at boot — so a workspace of marathon sessions can never quietly eat the disk, while with no config nothing is EVER auto-deleted.**
20:- **Config** (user file, tolerant section — unknown keys note, never boot-fail):
27:      sweep: boot           # boot | off — when the sweep runs (off = cap set but manual-only)
40:| Auto-delete | none (manual delete only) | boot sweep, oldest-first, exempt live/named/young |
49:- **AC1** — no `sessions:` section → no sweep ever runs, no boot note (byte-identical legacy; UT on config).
53:- **AC5** — deleting a session removes its sidecars (`.tools/`, `spills/<sid>/`, trajectory blobs) in the same unit (IT).
54:- **AC6** — `fa sessions clean --dry-run` prints the exact plan (paths, sizes, ages, reasons) and deletes nothing; apply performs exactly the plan (E2E).
55:- **AC7** — every auto-deletion emits one log line; a sweep report persists to the boot log (IT asserts the lines).
61:- `IT-1` — fake `ExecutionEnv` + fake clock: full sweep lifecycle incl. sidecars (AC3/5), log lines (AC7).
63:- `E2E-1` — real fs temp root: `fa sessions clean --dry-run` then apply; `/sessions` footer numbers (AC6).
73:- **E5** — `sweep: off` with cap set: manual verb still works, boot never sweeps (only the over-cap note).
86:1. Default posture: **B (recommended)** — no cap unless set, boot NOTE (no action) when total crosses a soft warn threshold (e.g. 5 GiB) suggesting the keys; or **A** — ship a generous default cap (e.g. 20 GiB) with the same exemptions, so hygiene exists out of the box. Owner decides.

