Concern: `_initializeSession` resolves `config.sessionName` right after — a named session is exempt via keepNamed ✓. An id-resume (`--session <id>`) — config.sessionName may carry an id too? The gh-1660 pattern is fire-and-forget unawaited — but the retention sweep DELETES files; if it runs concurrently with session open, a racy delete of the to-be-opened session... the presence row of THIS process registers at session open (heartbeat); during the sweep the row may already exist if open finished first — fail-closed skip. If sweep finishes first: deletes an old session this process is opening → _loadSession fails → CLI handles missing session (probably error). To be safe: AWAIT the retention check (it's stat-first, fast) rather than unawaited — the gh-1660 sweep is unawaited because trashing header-only files can't hurt an in-flight open (trash → rename). Deletion CAN hurt → await it. Slight boot delay (one stat walk) — acceptable (gh-1734 made boot fast but a stat walk over the sessions root is the same class of cost as cleanupEmptySessions... which they made unawaited. Hmm. But correctness first: await.)

Also — where is `config.sessionName`? There IS `config.sessionName` (line ~414). So currentSessionId protection: pass nothing; named resumes are exempt via name resolution (keepNamed) — but WAIT: name exemption resolves the session's NAME from its header (`_nameOf` pages back for the name record). `--session <id>` resume: no name → not exempt → deletable if old + over cap. To close the hole, pass `currentSessionId` when config.sessionName looks like a raw id (uuid-shaped). The sweper compares `session.sessionId == currentSessionId`. sessionName could be an id or a name. Ids are uuid-ish. The repo has sessionIdFromSessionPath / isSafePathSegment. Cheap check: if sessionName contains no '-' prefix slug pattern... session NAMES are free-form. Hmm: session ids format = timestamp_uuid? Earlier deletions showed names like '2026-10-21T19-54-22-126354_01a1607-....jsonl'. config.sessionName for id-resume would be the full id. Check how config.sessionName is consumed — `_sessionNameMatches(name)` matches BOTH ids and names? If it matches ids, then a match by id exists. Simplest robust approach: after the retention check, if the resolved session was deleted... overkill.

Decision: pass `currentSessionId: config.sessionName` only when it parses as a session id — is there a validator? `isSafePathSegment` + presence... Actually the sweper's currentSessionId equality is exact; passing a NAME there is harmless (no session id equals it). Passing an ID saves the race. A name-shaped string never equals an id → no harm. So just pass `currentSessionId: config.sessionName` unconditionally? If sessionName is a NAME, no id matches → no-op ✓. If it's an ID → protects ✓. The id check `session.sessionId == currentSessionId` — ids come from `sessionIdFromSessionPath` (file basename without .jsonl). What does `--session <id>` carry — the basename id? resolveSessionName handles prefixes... For the race to matter the user resumed by exact id; config.sessionName == that id (or prefix!). Prefix → no match → race remains for prefix resumes. Accept + document.

